    "seo_description": "Japanese-tit playback studies test call order and source. Their receiver responses are documented; the interpretation as syntax remains debated.",
    "finding": "Japanese great tits showed different receiver behaviors under component calls, natural versus reversed order, and single- versus two-source playback; a bounded novel cross-species sequence also elicited stronger responses than its reversal, while a matched ABCâ€“zi control was weak. In the 2022 deposited binary table, the natural single-source 1A-R condition had an approach event in 13/16 flock trials and wing-flicking in 15/16; the other three treatments had approach events in 2â€“3/16 and wing-flicking in 1â€“4/16. These aggregate counts reproduce the descriptive pattern only. They do not establish a unique mechanism: one published critique agrees that receiver behavior is sensitive to order and source but disputes that this demonstrates Core-Merge or hierarchical syntax.",
    "limitations": "These are species-, population- and task-bounded receiver-response results, not general animal-language or translation evidence. The 2017 novelty test combined a familiar heterospecific call type with Japanese-tit calls; it does not establish open-ended productivity, and its comparison with ABCâ€“D used earlier data from another year. The 2022 study extends the same research line in the same species, not an independent replication. Its two Figshare v1 outcome tables contain no audio and no location fields; dates and caller_ID fields remain in ignored local files. The current official Figshare API reports MD5 values and the two received data tables match them; this confirms byte agreement with the current provider manifest, not scientific validity or a data-to-figure reproduction. The committed summary contains aggregates only. Its script does not run the deposited R code or reproduce mixed-effects models and adjusted contrasts. Schlenker et al. (2024) offer two formal accounts that fit the source-separation result under different assumptions. Beckers et al. (2024) agree the evidence shows sensitivity to call order and distinguishable responses to 1AR versus 2AR, but argue that the experiment does not establish Core-Merge or hierarchical syntax; a finite-list account fits the 2022 result, and a finite-state/concatenation account is proposed for the broader order pattern. This is a published interpretive disagreement, not a new empirical result or consensus. The 2025 Carolina-wren/great-tit study is adjacent cross-family receiver-response evidence; it did not reverse order and is not a test or replication of Japanese-tit sequence-order sensitivity. No Talk2Nature playback or animal-facing experiment is proposed.",
    "lesson": "Report the measured receiver behavior and separate it from the theoretical label placed on the behavior. The 2022 source tables descriptively show a large difference between one natural same-source condition and the other tested conditions, but do not choose among Core-Merge, finite-list, concatenation or other mechanisms. A useful next review should assess the 2017 cross-species result alongside the 2016 and 2022 controls, and distinguish linear order, source linkage and hierarchical generalization. Any animal-facing study requires qualified scientific and welfare oversight; these results do not support an AI-generated verbal translation.",
