    "review_depth": "Read the Google / Herzing & Starner announcement and the current Google DeepMind DolphinGemma page, including its capability, training and community-release statements. Read the Wild Dolphin Project-hosted 2013 CHAT wearable-computer paper's protocol, recognizer design and reported pool/field evaluation. The current DeepMind page says DolphinGemma is still in development and will be openly available on release; it links no public checkpoint, technical paper, benchmark, or release date. No private partner status was checked, no model/software/data was downloaded or run, and no independent reproduction or specialist review occurred.",
    "finding": "DolphinGemma and CHAT answer different research questions. Google describes DolphinGemma as an approximately 400M-parameter audio sequence model that learns recurring patterns and predicts likely subsequent sound tokens from Atlantic spotted-dolphin recordings. The current official model page still lists it as in development and promises open availability on release, but provides no public checkpoint, model card, technical paper, benchmark or date; treat its capabilities as official project descriptions, not independently verified results. CHAT is a separate, researcher-designed interface that associates a small set of synthetic whistles with selected objects and recognizes possible mimics. The 2013 technical paper reports a purpose-built underwater device and controlled recognition tests; it does not decode spontaneous dolphin calls. Neither track demonstrates general translation.",
    "limitations": "DolphinGemma's current public page provides no reproducible evaluation or release artifact, so performance, rights and reuse readiness cannot be independently assessed. Its sequence-prediction objective can find regularities without identifying what a sound means. CHAT's synthetic signals are a taught interface, not decoded natural vocabulary; the 2013 paper's controlled recognition results do not establish broad comprehension or an animal-initiated, welfare-reviewed field outcome. Results from dolphins and underwater hydrophones do not transfer directly to parrots, consumer phones or other taxa.",
    "lesson": "Keep four evidence layers distinct in Talk2Nature: passive natural-call detection/representation; human-reviewed call categories; associations with independently observed behavior and social context; and receiver-response evidence from a separately approved experiment. A next-sound model can help propose patterns, but cannot by itself establish semantics. A learned-symbol interaction is a different protocol with its own welfare, consent, output and evaluation gates. For the current product, build passive, source-provenanced observations and human review; do not add animal-directed sound output or frame predictions as translations.",
    "next_review": "Recheck the official release page before considering DolphinGemma; review any eventual model card, exact checkpoint, license, evaluation split and training-data terms independently. For Talk2Nature, first define a passive acoustic task with valid labels and independent group-held-out evaluation. Keep any symbol-learning experiment out of the public companion until a qualified research team specifies protocol, animal-welfare review and receiver-response outcome.",
